1 REM ============================================================
2 REM  t57_virtual_devices.bpp
3 REM  BASIC++ Virtual Device System Test & Tutorial
4 REM ============================================================
5 REM
6 REM  BASIC++ implements a Virtual Device (VDev) system that
7 REM  emulates hardware peripherals found on retro computers.
8 REM  This abstraction layer lets programs access devices through
9 REM  a unified interface regardless of the host platform.
10 REM
11 REM  DEVICE INTROSPECTION COMMANDS:
12 REM    VDEV     - List all registered virtual devices
13 REM    VMEM     - Show virtual memory subsystem status
14 REM    VCON     - Show virtual console (screen) status
15 REM    VTERM    - Show virtual terminal status
16 REM    VMACH    - Show virtual machine (CPU emulation) status
17 REM    DEVMAP   - Show device-to-address mapping
18 REM    DEVICES  - List all active devices with their names
19 REM
20 REM  VDev CLASSES:
21 REM    VDCLASS_CONSOLE  - Screen/keyboard devices
22 REM    VDCLASS_STORAGE  - Disk/file devices
23 REM    VDCLASS_SERIAL   - Serial port devices
24 REM    VDCLASS_NETWORK  - Network devices
25 REM    VDCLASS_HID      - Human interface devices
26 REM    VDCLASS_AUDIO    - Sound devices
27 REM    VDCLASS_TIMER    - Timer/clock devices
28 REM    VDCLASS_GENERIC  - General-purpose devices
29 REM
30 DIALECT "GWBS"
31 REM
100 REM === VDEV - Virtual Device Listing ===
101 REM VDEV prints a table of all registered virtual devices
102 REM showing: name, class, capabilities, and version.
103 REM
104 REM Example output:
105 REM   Name      Class     Caps        Version
106 REM   CON:      Console   R/W/CTL     1.0
107 REM   LPT:      Stream    W           1.0
108 REM   USB:      Storage   R/CTL       1.0
109 REM   HID:      HID       R/BIN/CTL   1.0
110 TEST "VDEV listing"
120 VDEV
130 REM Lists all virtual devices
140 ENDTEST
200 REM === VMEM - Virtual Memory Status ===
201 REM VMEM shows the current memory subsystem:
202 REM   - Current MEMMAP (e.g., "MSDOS", "C64")
203 REM   - Virtual memory segment size (64K)
204 REM   - PEEK/POKE address range
205 REM   - DEF SEG setting
210 TEST "VMEM status"
220 VMEM
230 REM Should display memory configuration
240 ENDTEST
300 REM === VCON - Virtual Console Status ===
301 REM VCON shows the virtual console state:
302 REM   - Screen dimensions (WIDTH x HEIGHT)
303 REM   - Current cursor position (row, col)
304 REM   - Current text attributes (color, background)
305 REM   - Console mode (text/graphics)
310 TEST "VCON status"
320 VCON
330 REM Should display console state
340 ENDTEST
400 REM === VTERM - Virtual Terminal Status ===
401 REM VTERM shows the terminal emulation layer:
402 REM   - Terminal type (ANSI, VT100, etc.)
403 REM   - Escape sequence support level
404 REM   - Input mode (raw/cooked)
405 REM   - Character encoding (ASCII/UTF-8)
410 TEST "VTERM status"
420 VTERM
430 REM Should display terminal info
440 ENDTEST
500 REM === VMACH - Virtual Machine Status ===
501 REM VMACH shows the virtual machine state:
502 REM   - Interpreter version
503 REM   - Current dialect
504 REM   - Memory usage
505 REM   - Stack depth
510 TEST "VMACH status"
520 VMACH
530 REM Should display VM state
540 ENDTEST
600 REM === DEVMAP - Device Address Map ===
601 REM DEVMAP shows how virtual devices are mapped to
602 REM memory addresses, similar to I/O maps on real hardware.
603 REM On a C64, VIC-II is at $D000-$D3FF; on Atari, POKEY
604 REM is at $D200-$D20F. DEVMAP shows these mappings.
610 TEST "DEVMAP listing"
620 DEVMAP
630 REM Should display device address mappings
640 ENDTEST
700 REM === DEVICES ===
701 REM DEVICES prints a simple list of all available device
702 REM names that can be used with OPEN.
703 REM e.g.: CON: LPT: CAS: COM: USB: HID: USBSER: N:
710 TEST "DEVICES listing"
720 DEVICES
730 REM Should list all active device names
740 ENDTEST
9999 END
